{{also|Humbug}}
==English==

===Etymology===
First in use about 1735-40. Etymology {{unk.|lang=en|nocap=1}}. The {{w|Oxford English Dictionary}} states "The facts as to its origin appear to have been lost, even before the word became common enough to excite attention". Possibly from {{m|en|hummer||(slang) An obvious lie}} or from {{m|en|hum||(dialectal and slang) to delude, impose on, cajole}} + {{m|en|bug||a specter, goblin}}. {{w|John Camden Hotten}} suggests a link to the name of the German city of {{w|Hamburg}}, "from which town so many false bulletins and reports came during the war in the last century".
===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|slang}} A [[hoax]], [[prank]] or [[jest]].
# {{lb|en|slang}} A [[fraud]] or [[sham]].
# {{lb|en|slang}} [[nonsense|Nonsense]].
# {{lb|en|slang}} A [[fraudster]], [[cheat]], or [[hypocrite]].
#* '''1903''', [[w:George Bernard Shaw|George Bernard Shaw]], ''[[w:Man and Superman|Man and Superman]]'', Act III:
#*: He means that he has sold out to the parliamentary '''humbugs''' and the bourgeoisie. Compromise! that is his faith. 
# {{lb|en|US slang}} Anything worrying, complicated, unpleasant, offensive, troublesome or a misunderstanding, especially if trivial.
# {{lb|en|US|African American Vernacular|slang}} A fight.
# {{lb|en|US|African American Vernacular|slang|dated}} A gang.
# {{lb|en|US|crime|slang}} A false arrest on trumped-up charges.
# {{lb|en|British}} A type of [[chewy]] [[sweet]] ([[candy]]).

